They all (at least all I can think of) come from either the mythology of
some country in our world or some fictionary work.

Examples:

Excalibur of course comes from the Legend of Arthur Pendragon.

Mjollnir comes from a Norse legend; it was originally Tor's (a god)
hammer.

Lich... Wait, let me see... Hmm, can't find anything on the background
just now.

The One Ring (ever #chatted with a hobbit?) of course comes from the
Lord of the Rings; as do Orcs and a host of others.

The word 'dwarves' (plural for dwarf, as opposed to the grammatically
correct 'dwarfs') also comes from LotR. Same thing with 'elves'.

Ask me more!

Some good references:
 - almost any decent book on mythology
 - The Advanced Dungeons&Dragons 2nd Edition Monstrous Compendium
   (volumes 1&2 should be enough)
 - other AD&D books (other fantasy role-playing games should do)
 - of course, the fictionary works; i.e. The Lord of the Rings

The AD&D Monstrous Compendiums are great! There is a postcard-sized
drawing of each monster, lot of stats (some of which might be taken into
Nethack from exactly that book; at least the Str, Dex, Int, Wis, ...
names of the abilities are), a description of the monster, its habits,
ecology, combat info, ... Of course, it's somewhat RPG related but still
good.

